00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:59.400
complicated linguistics, but there are a lot&nbsp;
of different ways to make a written language.

00:05:59.400 --> 00:06:04.020
For example, English and other romantic&nbsp;
languages are made up from alphabets.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.020 --> 00:06:07.320
They have letters that you can put&nbsp;
together to form syllables or words,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.320 --> 00:06:10.380
and then you can use those to&nbsp;
make up more complicated ideas.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:14.880
But that's not the only way to make a written&nbsp;
language. Another major way is by using logograms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:14.880 --> 00:06:19.380
where different symbols represent individual&nbsp;
words. Kanji in Japanese and ancient Egyptian&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.380 --> 00:06:24.000
hieroglyphics: Those are examples of logographic&nbsp;
writing systems. But if you take it one step&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:28.860
further than logograms, you get ideograms, which&nbsp;
are symbols meant to represent entire ideas.

00:06:28.860 --> 00:06:32.160
So stuff like numbers or the&nbsp;
symbols that we use in math,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:32.160 --> 00:06:36.420
those are ideograms. The number “1”&nbsp;
represents the entire concept of one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.420 --> 00:06:39.900
just like the + symbol represents&nbsp;
the entire concept of addition.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:46.260
Languages made up from ideograms are able to tell&nbsp;
very complex stories with relatively few symbols.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.260 --> 00:06:52.860
If you want an example, we can look at UNLWS or&nbsp;
the Unker Non-Linear Writing System. Using this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.860 --> 00:06:57.600
we can break this big squiggly line into&nbsp;
smaller squiggly lines, each with their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.600 --> 00:07:04.020
individual meaning like “me” or “eats”. But put&nbsp;
together this symbol means “I eat a large fish”.

